## Modeling Game Difficulty Levels

Difficulty levels form a clear progression: easier modes give more help, harder modes give less.

### The Difficulty Enum

```xiom
enum Difficulty {
  Easy,
  Medium,
  Hard,
  Expert,
}
```

### Maximum Attempts Per Level

Easier modes allow more attempts before game over:

```xiom
fn Difficulty.max_attempts(self) -> Int {
  match self {
    Easy => { return 10; }
    Medium => { return 5; }
    Hard => { return 3; }
    Expert => { return 1; }
  }
}
```

### Describing Each Level

```xiom
fn Difficulty.description(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Easy => { return "Relaxed � plenty of chances to learn"; }
    Medium => { return "Standard � a fair challenge"; }
    Hard => { return "Tough � mistakes are costly"; }
    Expert => { return "Brutal � one error and you're done"; }
  }
}
```

### Selecting Difficulty

```xiom
let difficulty = Difficulty.Hard;
difficulty.max_attempts();   // 3
difficulty.description();    // "Tough � mistakes are costly"
```

### Comparing Difficulty Levels

```xiom
fn attempts_remaining(d: Difficulty, used: Int) -> Int {
  let max = d.max_attempts();
  if used >= max {
    return 0;
  }
  return max - used;
}
```

### Using Difficulty in Game Logic

```xiom
let level = Difficulty.Medium;
var used = 3;
attempts_remaining(level, 3);  // 2 (5 max - 3 used)
attempts_remaining(level, 6);  // 0 (used exceeds max)
```

